# Objects: grouping values and the functions over them.
# Origin: REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3, section 10.
#
# Written before objects exist. Milestone 2 in docs/milestones.md, and a
# dependency of nearly everything after it: a VID face is an object, so
# `face/color` in the demo sources is field access on one.

# --- Making --------------------------------------------------

--- id objects/make-object-holds-its-fields
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
example: make object! [
    var1: 10
    var2: 20
]
example/var1
--- result
10

--- id objects/a-field-can-be-computed-from-an-earlier-one
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object op
--- note The body is evaluated in order, so a later field can use an earlier
--- note one. That is what makes it a block being run rather than a literal.
--- code
example: make object! [
    var1: 10
    var2: var1 + 10
]
example/var2
--- result
20

--- id objects/an-empty-object-is-legal
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
empty: make object! []
type? empty
--- result
#(object!)

--- id objects/two-objects-from-one-body-are-separate
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- note Each MAKE runs the body again, so the fields are new rather than
--- note shared. Without this, one instance would tread on another.
--- code
body: [count: 0]
first-one: make object! body
second-one: make object! body
first-one/count: 99
second-one/count
--- result
0

# --- Reading and writing fields ------------------------------

--- id objects/a-field-can-be-assigned-through-a-path
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
account: make object! [balance: 0]
account/balance: 1000
account/balance
--- result
1000

--- id objects/assigning-a-field-does-not-add-one
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- note An object has the fields its body gave it. Assigning to a name it
--- note does not have is a mistake rather than a way to extend it.
--- code
account: make object! [balance: 0]
account/nosuchfield: 1
--- error script invalid-path

--- id objects/reading-a-field-that-is-not-there-raises
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
account: make object! [balance: 0]
account/nosuchfield
--- error script invalid-path

--- id objects/in-gives-the-word-in-the-objects-context
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
account: make object! [balance: 1000]
get in account 'balance
--- result
1000

--- id objects/set-in-writes-through-the-word
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- note Reaching a field by a word held in a variable rather than by a
--- note literal path, which is how a field name can be worked out at
--- note runtime.
--- code
account: make object! [balance: 1000]
set in account 'balance 20
account/balance
--- result
20

--- id objects/fields-can-be-read-by-a-word-held-in-a-variable
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object series control output
--- code
person: make object! [first-name: "Fred" last-name: "Smith"]
foreach word [first-name last-name] [prin get in person word]
--- prints
FredSmith

# --- Methods -------------------------------------------------

--- id objects/a-function-in-an-object-reaches-its-fields
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object control op
--- note This is the point of an object rather than a block: the function
--- note sees the fields beside it without being handed them.
--- code
counter: make object! [
    count: 0
    step: does [count: count + 1]
]
counter/step
counter/step
counter/count
--- result
2

--- id objects/a-method-takes-arguments
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object control op
--- code
account: make object! [
    balance: 0
    deposit: func [amount] [balance: balance + amount]
]
account/deposit 100
account/deposit 10
account/balance
--- result
110

--- id objects/methods-on-two-instances-do-not-share-state
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object control op
--- code
maker: [count: 0 step: does [count: count + 1]]
one: make object! maker
other: make object! maker
one/step
other/count
--- result
0

# --- Deriving ------------------------------------------------

--- id objects/make-from-an-object-copies-it
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
original: make object! [var1: 10]
copied: make original []
copied/var1
--- result
10

--- id objects/a-derived-object-can-add-fields
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
original: make object! [var1: 10]
extended: make original [var2: 20]
extended/var2
--- result
20

--- id objects/a-derived-object-does-not-share-the-original
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- note Deriving copies rather than linking, so changing the child leaves
--- note the parent alone. REBOL objects have no live inheritance.
--- code
original: make object! [var1: 10]
derived: make original []
derived/var1: 99
original/var1
--- result
10

# --- Context -------------------------------------------------

--- id objects/context-is-make-object-without-the-ceremony
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- code
settings: context [width: 640 height: 480]
settings/width
--- result
640

--- id objects/an-object-does-not-leak-into-the-caller
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object
--- note The fields are the object's, not the enclosing script's, which is
--- note what makes an object a way of not colliding.
--- code
width: "outer"
settings: context [width: 640]
width
--- result
"outer"

--- id objects/a-field-can-see-out-to-the-enclosing-context
--- origin adapted from REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object control
--- note A word the object does not define falls through to where the body
--- note was written, which is how a method can call an ordinary function.
--- code
example: make object! [
    doubled: multiply 21 2
]
example/doubled
--- result
42

--- id objects/an-object-can-be-molded
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires object
--- note Every object holds a word for itself, so molding one used to
--- note recurse until the stack gave out. SELF is left out of the printed
--- note form for that reason, and REBOL leaves it out for the same one.
--- note One field per line, indented four. This entry recorded the flat
--- note form for a long time, on the strength of having been read off
--- note JEBOL's own output rather than the binary's -- which is what its
--- note origin said, and why an origin is worth reading.
--- code
settings: make object! [width: 640]
mold settings
--- result
"make object! [^/    width: 640^/]"

--- id objects/self-refers-to-the-object
--- origin REBOL/Core User Guide 2.3 section 10
--- requires object control
--- note It is still there and still usable, just not printed.
--- code
thing: make object! [
    size: 10
    mine: does [self]
]
same-thing: thing/mine
same-thing/size
--- result
10

--- id objects/a-copied-method-acts-on-the-copy
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires object control op
--- note The entry above proves a copied *field* is independent. This proves
--- note a copied *method* is, which is a different thing and was broken: the
--- note function still closed over the object it was written in, so
--- note depositing into the copy moved money in the original.
--- code
account: make object! [
    balance: 0
    deposit: func [amount] [balance: balance + amount]
]
account/deposit 100
savings: make account []
savings/deposit 10
rejoin [account/balance " and " savings/balance]
--- result
"100 and 110"

--- id objects/two-copies-have-independent-methods
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- requires object control op
--- code
maker: make object! [
    count: 0
    step: does [count: count + 1]
]
one: make maker []
other: make maker []
one/step
one/step
other/step
rejoin [one/count " and " other/count]
--- result
"2 and 1"
